The file has only three commits touching it: the creation commit (3545223688), the commit that added `loadUserTypes` (b45c4545d2), and the fix (a4ec18f80). That narrows things down cleanly.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: b45c4545d2f4027e77dfb4fe856c42f51d897c08

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the history of `src/java/com/scylladb/tools/BulkLoader.java` with `git log --follow` and found only two prior commits touching the file: the initial file creation (354522368) and b45c4545d2, which added the `loadUserTypes` method itself (along with the whole UDT-loading feature). I then diffed b45c4545d2 to see the original implementation.

**(2) Specific defect:** b45c4545d2 introduced:
```java
private static void loadUserTypes(Collection<UserType> udts, KSMetaData ks) {
    LinkedList<UserType> notLoaded = new LinkedList<UserType>();
    for (UserType ut : udts) { ... }
    if (notLoaded.size() == udts.size()) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Unable to load user types " + notLoaded);
    }
    if (notLoaded.size() > 0) {
        loadUserTypes(notLoaded, ks);
    }
}
```
When `udts` is empty (e.g., a keyspace with no user-defined types), `notLoaded.size()` is `0`, which equals `udts.size()` (`0`), so the method incorrectly throws `RuntimeException("Unable to load user types []")` even though there was nothing to fail on. The fix in a4ec18f80 adds an early-return guard (`if (udts.isEmpty()) return;`) at the top of the method to handle this base case, and simplifies the recursive call accordingly (safe because the next recursion level now short-circuits on empty input instead of re-triggering the same miscomparison).

**(3) Evidence:** The diff of b45c4545d2 shows the exact `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` / `notLoaded.size() > 0` logic that the fix commit replaces, and this is the commit that introduced the method — there is no earlier version of this code to consider. The call site `loadUserTypes(keyspaceMetadata.getUserTypes(), ksMetaData);` added in the same commit means any keyspace without UDTs would immediately hit this bug.

I'm confident in this attribution — the method and the faulty base-case logic were both introduced together in a single commit, with no intervening changes.

